---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Install from '../components/Install.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import Examples from '../components/Examples.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div class="hero-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow"><i></i> ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAMS FOR DEVELOPERS</span><span class="version mono">OPEN SOURCE / v0.1</span></div>
      <h1 id="hero-title">A little opinionated.<br /><span>A lot clearer<span class="hero-period">.</span></span></h1>
      <div class="hero-bottom">
        <div class="hero-description"><p>Use a coding agent for the first draft.<br class="desktop-break" /> Refine it in Studio. Keep the source in a plain text file.</p><p class="agent-note">Use Codex, Claude Code, Cursor, Antigravity, or another coding agent. Set it up separately.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('docs/')}>See how it works <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
        <div class="hero-start"><Install /></div>
      </div>
      <Terminal />
    </section>

    <div class="principles-strip" aria-label="Product principles"><span>WHAT YOU GET</span><p>Agent-compatible</p><b aria-hidden="true">+</b><p>Local by default</p><b aria-hidden="true">+</b><p>Versioned with Git</p><b aria-hidden="true">+</b><p>Open source</p></div>

    <Examples />

    <section class="manifesto section" data-reveal aria-labelledby="manifesto-title">
      <div class="manifesto-main"><span class="eyebrow">02 / A FORMAT YOU CAN REVIEW</span><h2 id="manifesto-title">Your diagram should explain the system.<br /><em>Not become another system to maintain.</em></h2><p>There is no hosted workspace to manage or hidden layout state to track. You get a readable file, a deterministic engine, and control over the final result.</p><a class="button light" href={url('docs/how-it-works/')}>See how it works <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      <div class="manifesto-notes"><article><span class="mono">.dgm</span><h3>The file is the source of truth.</h3><p>The structure and saved visual choices live together. Studio edits that document; it does not replace it.</p></article><article><span class="mono">1 → 1</span><h3>Repeatable output.</h3><p>The same source, catalog, and saved view produce the same layout, routes, and SVG.</p></article><article><span class="mono">AWS FIRST</span><h3>A focused scope.</h3><p>The project focuses on architecture diagrams. AWS has the broadest grammar and example coverage today.</p></article></div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq section" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">03 / PRACTICAL QUESTIONS</span><h2 id="faq-title">What to expect.</h2><p>A focused tool with defined boundaries.</p></div><div class="faq-list">
      <details><summary>Do I need a coding agent?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>No. A coding agent is optional. If you use one, choose Codex, Claude Code, Cursor, Antigravity, or another tool that fits your workflow. Dumb Diagrams provides project instructions and examples, not an agent or model access. You can write .dgm files yourself or edit them in Studio.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Where do my diagrams live?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>On your machine, as .dgm files. Start Studio through the CLI to read and save those files directly. The standalone browser editor uses browser storage and explicit downloads instead. <a href={url('docs/studio/')}>Read about the two modes.</a></p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I use it for clouds other than AWS?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>The catalog also includes Azure, Google Cloud, and cloud-agnostic assets. AWS currently has the strongest grammar, examples, and visual guidance. Support and coverage vary by provider.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How is this different from a whiteboard?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Dumb Diagrams models services, boundaries, and connections, then applies a defined layout that you can refine. It is not a general illustration tool, live infrastructure inventory, or hosted multiplayer workspace.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is it free and open source?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Yes. The project is released under AGPL-3.0-only. Provider icons retain their own upstream terms. You can read the <a href={url('LICENSE.txt')}>project license</a> and <a href={url('ICON-NOTICES.txt')}>icon notices</a>.</p></details>
    </div></section>

    <section class="closing" data-reveal><div><span class="eyebrow">START WITH ONE DIAGRAM</span><h2>Put the architecture<br /><em>on the page.</em></h2></div><a href={url('docs/quick-start/')} class="closing-cta">Create your first diagram <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
  </main>
</Base>
